n, influencing later cultural and literary movements.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-09-07T07:04:08Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#34091398226", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Pal Engjëlli was an Albanian prelate of the Roman Catholic Church who served as archbishop of Durrës from 1460 to 1469. In 1462 he wrote the oldest known text in Albanian.", "newValue": "Pal Engjëlli (1416–1470), a distinguished Albanian prelate of the Roman Catholic Church, holds a pivotal place in both ecclesiastical and linguistic history, primarily for authoring the oldest known text in the Albanian language. Serving as Archbishop of Durrës from 1460 to 1469, his life unfolded amidst the tumultuous backdrop of Ottoman expansion into the Balkans and the heroic resistance led by Skanderbeg, positioning him as a significant figure in the spiritual and political landscape of 15th-century Albania.

His most enduring contribution is the *Formula e Pagëzimit* (Baptismal Formula), a brief liturgical phrase written in 1462. Discovered in a pastoral letter within a Latin manuscript by Romanian scholar Nicolae Iorga in 1915, this simple sentence – 'Un'te paghesont premenit Atit e Birit e Spertit Senit' ('I baptize you in the name of the Father and the Son and the Holy Spirit') – provides the earliest concrete evidence of written Albanian. Beyond its linguistic significance, Engjëlli's work reflects the practical pastoral needs of a Latin rite church ministering to a vernacular-speaking populace in an era when oral tradition dominated for most languages.

